Day 245/250:  Minimum Difference Between Highest and Lowest of K Scores

Problem Statement #1984

Problem Statement EXplanation:

You are given a 0-indexed integer array nums, where nums[i] represents the score of the ith student. You are also given an integer k.
Pick the scores of any k students from the array so that the difference between the highest and the lowest of the k scores is minimized.
Return the minimum possible difference.

Solution Approach:

To find the minimum difference efficiently, we use a Sorting + Sliding Window approach:
Sorting: By sorting the array in ascending order, we ensure that any group of k students with the "closest" scores will be located adjacent to each other.
Sliding Window: We slide a window of size k across the sorted array.
The first element in the window (nums[i]) is the minimum score.
The last element in the window (nums[i + k - 1]) is the maximum score.
Calculation: For each window, we calculate nums[i + k - 1] - nums[i] and update our global minimum.

Here's the code:

import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int minimumDifference(int[] nums, int k) {
        if (k == 1) return 0;
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        int minDiff = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int i = 0; i <= nums.length - k; i++) {
            minDiff = Math.min(minDiff, nums[i + k - 1] - nums[i]);
        }
        return minDiff;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(nlogn)
Space Complexity: O(1) or O(logn)
